  "problem": "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=2{e}^{4x}+x,$ then $f=O({e}^{4x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,2{e}^{4x}=O(2{e}^{4x})$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{2{e}^{4x}})=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,x=O(2{e}^{4x})$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(4{e}^{4x})$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O({e}^{4x})$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}.",
